+/* Camera rate: the camera update keeps the 30 Hz step too.
+ *
+ * Reported: at 60 FPS the skull platform in Air Crash sent the camera back to
+ * the start of the level, where it stayed. The platform is the end of the
+ * secret jet-board section (zones S3-S9, then S0), and its warp is a handshake
+ * the camera takes part in:
+ *
+ *   - the platform (WarpC, subtype 9) sends Crash event 0x1600. His state 65
+ *     spins him, points the camera at the spin (global 4 bit 0x20000, target
+ *     0x8006CD70), fades to black, then queues camera event 0x10;
+ *   - the camera matches 0x10 against the 0x1A8 records of the path it is on
+ *     (0x80026334). Only S0's has one: at its end, "prepare a cut to link 1",
+ *     zone 04's path at its start. Preparing it (0x8002655C) sends Crash the
+ *     0x198 event of that path - 0x3200 with x, y, z - and his state 39 puts
+ *     him there and queues "cut now" (0x400). Cut and teleport land together.
+ *
+ * Until then the camera follows its path toward the spin, and that motion
+ * (0x80023E60, from the kind-0 handler 0x8002271C) is a step per CALL with no
+ * frame-time term. Run once per field, the camera moved twice as far in the
+ * same time. S0's end links into zones 03 and 04 - the level's start - so a
+ * camera that reaches it before the fade is over leaves S0, and event 0x10
+ * then finds no record on the path it is on: no cut, no teleport, and nothing
+ * to follow where it went.
+ *
+ * So the camera update now runs on script steps only, like the scripts it
+ * exchanges messages and events with, and every per-call rate inside it - path
+ * motion, the mode-6 blend countdown, the look-at smoothing - is the stock
+ * one. A recompile-time word (game.toml c2-60-cam-flag) turns the load of the
+ * crash pointer at 0x80026D04 into a load of a flag this hook stores at sp+44
+ * of the 48-byte frame, its unused padding: the crash pointer on a script step
+ * - the very word the original loads - and 0 on a physics-only field, which
+ * takes the update's own "no crash" exit. With the mode off the flag is always
+ * the crash pointer and the function is the original.
+ *
+ * Drawn once per field, that camera would move at 30 Hz under 60 Hz objects.
+ * On a physics-only field the frame is drawn from a pose extrapolated from the
+ * last two updates by the share of a step since the last one, and the real
+ * pose goes back before the next loop runs anything, so the game's own logic
+ * only ever sees poses its camera update made. Nothing is extrapolated across
+ * a cut (0x8005B99B set by the update), a jump larger than camera motion, or
+ * without the poses of two consecutive steps.
+ * PSX_CRASH2_60FPS_CAMERA_RATE=0 brings back the per-field camera for A/B. */
+#define C2_60_CAM_UPDATE     0x80026CA0u  /* the camera update, once per loop */
+#define C2_60_CAM_FLAG_SLOT  4u           /* below the caller's sp: its sp+44 */
+#define C2_60_CAM_POSE       0x800607F4u  /* x, y, z (world << 8), angles x, y, z */
+#define C2_60_CAM_WORDS      6u
+#define C2_60_CAM_CUT        0x8005B99Bu  /* byte: "cut now", reset by each update */
+#define C2_60_CAM_MAX_MOVE   (2048 << 8)  /* per step and axis; more is a cut */
+#define C2_60_CAM_MAX_TURN   512          /* per step and axis, of 4096 */
 
